 50th: 47, 75th: 53, Max: 63\n\n 3_2 Min: 35, 25th: 42, 50th: 47, 75th: 52, Max: 62\n\n 3_3 Min: 34, 25th: 42, 50th: 47, 75th: 51, Max: 62\n\n Min: 1, 25th: 18,\n 4 50th: 31, 75th: 45, Max: 61","Summary\n\n 14","Summary\n• The goal of these analyses was to determine whether the\n growth model outlined in the introduction provides\n reasonable and fair growth goals for students\n• A reasonable target is one that provides a decent chance of\n actually achieving the goal.\n• A fair target is one that does not markedly differ based upon\n the formulation of the model.","Summary: Reasonableness\n• In general, the probability of students reaching their growth goal\n appears to be reasonable.\n • Transition probabilities in ELA ranged between 0.48 and 0.70 of\n achieving the growth goal. In mathematics, the probabilities\n ranged between 0.4 and 0.7.\n • Median SGPs ranged from 38 to 51 in ELA and from 31 to 61 in\n mathematics.\n• Results from both models confirm that on average, the goals for\n students are reasonable.","Summary: Fairness\n• Fairness looks at the consistency of the AGPs across prior achievement levels as well\n as consistency of the AGPs within each of the prior achievement levels.\n • The results suggest that growth goals for higher achieving students is slightly\n easier than for lower achieving students.\n • Such a result is consistent with what has been found in several states that\n students who are behind need to do more to catch-up.\n • In some prior achievement levels (e.g., 1_1), there is a rather wide range of AGPs\n necessary to reach the growth goal. This suggest much greater difficulty for\n students at the bottom of the achievement level and much easier difficulty for\n students at the top.\n • In some cases, the targets appear unreasonable. Average across all students\n within a school, the results will likely “average” out and not be of concern.","Additional Results\n\n 18","Transition Probabilities\nGrades 4/ELA\n Current Achievement Lev